[Music]  hi thanks for coming in checking out  chess openings abridged this is gonna be  a short video series where we go over  the chess openings in a shorter amount  of time taking over Jonathan trounces  legacy today we're gonna be going over  the Sicilian defense the dragon  variation in the Yugoslav attack we're  gonna go over the main line and get  cement of the main attacking ideas for  both sides so with it being the Sicilian  defense we first start with E for c5  following the main lines we end up  playing Knight of 3 d6 and then d4 and  through best practice white gives up a  center pawn in order to get piece  activity we play Knight of 6 and Knight  c3 to defend our a pawn and then what  characterizes it as the Dragon is this  move g6 here there are other  alternatives black has such as a6 the  NIE Dorf  there's also east 6 the shivahn again  you have the classical with nice e6 but  again we're just gonna be focusing on  the dragon the next move is Bishop e3  and this is what it helps turn it into  the Yugoslavia attack we want to make  sure all of our pieces are being  developed and Bishop e3 gets our dark  square Bishop out we play Bishop g7 to  Fein kettle or King side Bishop and then  we play f3 we play off 3 to stop the  move Knight g4 the reason we don't need  to play off 3 right away is because if  we go back and black plays Knight g4 in  this position it's actually a big-time  blunder  we have Bishop b5 check and black ends  up losing a piece if he ends up going  Bishop to d7 we can end up taking on g4  with the Queen because our Bishop is  pinning blacks Bishop if they block with  the night we take with our knight and  black runs into a fork and loses the  exchange so black can't play Knight g4  right away  back to our position after f3 black  castles Queen d2 we're looking to castle  on the Queen side knight c6 developing a  knight and now here White has two  alternatives they have Bishop c4 or they  have castles we're gonna be focusing on  Bishop to c4 so we can understand the  main ideas for both sides Bishop d7  continuing to develop Castle queenside  and here whenever both sides Castle on  opposite sides of the board we have  White King on the Queen side we have the  black King on the Kings side both sides  are gonna be rushing to push their pawns  to attack the enemies King we're gonna  try and break down their fortresses  we're gonna play rook c8 and then  Sicilian this is a very common idea we  want to put a rook on c8 to take control  of an open C file Bishop b3 is a little  bit of prophylactic prophylaxis against  Knight e5 because we don't want to move  this bishop with temp but we can just  move it right away 95 now we're gonna go  ahead with the idea of pushing pawns to  break down the fortress  h4 black is gonna play Knight c4 he's  gonna trade his knight for one of  White's bishops so if Y allows it he's  going to take on e3 but why it's  actually gonna take on c4 with the  bishop we're gonna take on c4 with the  rook we don't want to move the Queen we  have a nice little battery coming down  here with the Queen and Bishop here  white is going to go with a pawn  sacrifice this is actually a big theme  in the Yugoslavs  we can sacrifice a pawn to open up the H  file the Knights gonna take we don't  want to ruin our pawn structure and then  White's gonna play g4 and attack the  knight so we have to move it back to f6  and here now we see White has an open H  file he's trying to attack now black has  a very common sacrifice that we're about  to see in a couple moves why is the play  h6 and trying try  off this dragon bishop they say this  bishop and the dragon is very very  important for black and sometimes can be  considered worth the value of a rook  which is five points black is going to  go and take on e4 with the knight and  this might seem strange at first because  it looks like we're just taking upon and  if they take our knight then it looks  like we just gave up a knight but we  actually had this move rook takes d4  attacking the Queen and after the Queen  moves we can actually recover pretty  nicely after rook takes check and here  both sides are doing fine now let's say  they move the Queen or common move you  might see is queen to h2 but this lets  black kind of win after Bishop e5 our  queen is being attacked so the best move  is Queen to e3 just so that way there's  no tempos off the Queen and the common  sacrifice for black is rook takes on c3  this is a positional sacrifice after B  takes c3 a very common move is Queen to  a5 trying to come in and break in on a  White's Queen side and if they take with  the Queen then while the Queen hangs so  they can't do that but B takes c3 our  knight is under attack so you have to  move back to f6 and now we see why it  has this attack on the Kings side coming  towards this King Black is gonna try and  attack on White's Queen side and this is  kind of where the middle game begins we  exit out of the opening so this is the  Yugoslav attack again the key features  is going to be black sacrificing on c3  for position and it's going to be a  hyper aggressive game with both sides  trying to checkmate each other so that  is the Sicilian defense dragon variation  in the Yugoslav attack  [Music]

